Keep the registered tunnel until the UE has registered over the new one.

ims_ipsec_pcscf kept its one tunnel in security_temp, so a re-challenge
replaced the working tunnel the moment the 401 went out - patch 0003 tears the
old one down right there - and the 401 itself, sent from the old protected
server port, left with no policy to cover it. A UE that then fails or abandons
the second REGISTER has no usable SA at all until the contact expires, and
every MT request in between goes over SAs it may not have installed yet. TS
33.203 7.4 has it the other way round: the SA set a 401 offers is only a
candidate until a REGISTER over it is answered 200.

Now security_temp holds the offer and security the tunnel the UE is registered
over, both written through to the database:

- ipsec_create() builds the offer beside the registered tunnel. An earlier
  offer the UE never registered over is superseded and removed, as 0003 did.
  The registered tunnel is removed up front only when the two would be the same
  kernel state somewhere - an SA is keyed by destination and SPI, a policy by
  its ports - which is what a UE that keeps its SPIs across offers, against
  33.203, does. Then the new one has to win at once, or it would be installed
  with the old one's keys (the add fails EEXIST): that is the old behaviour,
  kept for that case only.
- ipsec_promote(), exported in the API for ims_registrar_pcscf, makes the offer
  the registered tunnel when a REGISTER came in over it - from the UE's
  protected client port to the offer's protected server port - removes the
  tunnel it replaces and releases that pair. Anything else promotes nothing.
- ipsec_forward() sends MT requests over the registered tunnel, the offer
  before a first registration completes, and a reply over the tunnel its
  request came in on.
- Expiry, ipsec_destroy() and ipsec_destroy_by_contact() remove both.

Until ims_registrar_pcscf calls ipsec_promote() nothing fills security, and
every 401 replaces the previous offer as 0003 did.

The security_t around a REGISTER's Security-Client was leaked on every
registration; it is freed or handed to the contact now.

